Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:02.790 align:start position:0%
 
you're watching the daily climate show  on  sky news on today's programme an extreme  heat wave  grips parts of north america with cities  sweltering  under record-breaking temperatures how  retrofitting could be the key to  decarbonizing  commercial buildings and is the sports  industry playing ball  when it comes to tackling climate change  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions now an extreme heat wave has  hit parts of northwestern united  states and canada breaking all-time  temperature records  the u.s national weather service has  issued excessive heat warnings across  several states  unprepared for such hot weather  conditions  so let's take a look then what's going  on and for weeks now parts of the u.s  have been  experiencing extreme heat with  southwestern states reporting  dangerously hot temperatures well now  another heat wave  has hit north america breaking  temperature records  across the pacific northwest and western  canada  well parts of portland in oregon reached  43.3 degrees celsius on sunday  beating the all-time record of 42.2  degrees which was set just a day earlier  then in seattle  where only one third of households have  air conditioning  temperatures hit 39.4 degrees  celsius at the city's international  airport now that's the first time the  area had recorded  two consecutive days of heat above 37.7  degrees  according to the u.s national weather  service and then in canada the village  of litton in british columbia  reached 46.1 degrees on sunday a new  all-time  high temperature for the country and the  previous record of 45 degrees  was set in 1937. well forecasters say  even hotter temperatures are predicted  for the coming days  so what does this mean for people who  are living there well let's bring in our  u.s correspondent greg milan who's in  palmdale in california  so greg what kind of impact is this  extreme heat having there  well it's having a real impact we're  actually on the southern side of that  that heat dome that high pressure  weather system that is sitting over  the western united states actually  palmdale last week saw its  record june temperature since the 1960s  and that record will be broken again  today so it's a huge swathe of the  western united states that's being  affected and those  numbers you mentioned up in washington  state and in oregon really are  astonishing because those are places  that are generally pretty temperate  that that record you mentioned in in  portland 42 degrees on saturday 44 on  sunday  the average temperature in june in  portland is 22 degrees  so that shows you how bad things are  there uh seattle has never seen  three consecutive days of 100 degrees  fahrenheit so they're seeing remarkable  temperatures  and what the climate scientists are  saying is that of course you can't link  one  weather event to climate change but this  is part of a trend that the north west  is warming  they're seeing increasing numbers of  these hot days and have over the last  few years  and they say we will see more of these  systems as the weather patterns change  and therefore more heat waves in  in more places the impacts are really  direct here in particular they talk  about the night time temperature because  although it's hot in the day  it's not really dropping a great deal at  night and that's they say that adds a  real stress  to the body the heat stress and health  related problems that come from  not really being able to recover from  the heat up in the northwest they're  trying what they can with cooling  centers opening up the libraries for  people to go and  get that air conditioning because as you  say many people simply  don't have it they're having to slow the  trains down because the tracks are  warping  uh in the heat and then there are the  bigger issues there's been an extreme  drought across the west  that is getting worse that brings an  increased fire risk of course they're  seeing fires  acting like they normally would at the  end of a summer  it is a real concern here and while  there's some relief in sight here at  least  that weather system is is moving east so  other places will experience this  they say we should all get used to this  in the future greg thank you  in today's other climate news the  european parliament has approved its new  eu climate law making the target to  reach net zero by 2050 a binding  obligation  the law increases the eu's target for  reduction of greenhouse gas emissions  from 40 percent  to at least 55 percent by 2030.  it also includes a commitment to reach  negative carbon emissions  after 2050. the scottish qualifications  authority has been criticized for  including the positives of climate  change in its geography syllabus  it includes tourism in northern england  being boosted by higher temperatures  as one of the so-called benefits of a  changing climate  the exam body has since released a  statement saying the ability to take a  contrary view  is essential to some careers  scientists are warning that woodland  plants that rely on birds for seed  dispersal  may struggle to adapt to climate change  a study by the university of exeter has  found  plants in the northern hemisphere will  need to migrate to cooler areas further  north  due to a warming climate it suggests  almost 90 percent of plant species  have their seeds dispersed by birds who  take them south  areas that are becoming too warm for  these plants to grow  eco scores will be appearing on some  food and drinks packaging  from this autumn the traffic light  system will rank the environmental  impact of food and drink  and will be trialled by british brands  like markson spencer and sainsbury's  the scheme created by foundation earth  hopes to encourage customers to make  more environmentally friendly choices  and olympia neil aegis has set off on a  world  record-breaking open water swim he aims  to cover 150 kilometers with no stops  and no  sleep swimming all the way from linosa  island just off africa  to malta neil says he wants to draw  attention to  ocean plastic pollution having himself  nearly been killed by a plastic bag  while swimming  at night now there's a lot of focus on  energy efficiency and sustainability  when it comes to new  building projects but constructing  commercial facilities  can be very carbon intensive one way of  reducing energy use and carbon emissions  in existing offices  shops and warehouses is through retrofit  experts say retrofitting needs to be a  big part of the government's new heat  and building strategy  expected to be released in the coming  months so  here's everything you need to know about  the process in 60 seconds  [Music]  so  [Applause]  well joining me now is juliette morgan  head of sustainable development at  british land welcome to you now you've  been involved in retrofitting  a big building on your campus in london  first of all why did you decide to do  that and not just knock it down and  start all over again  um thanks for the question we made a  commitment a number of years ago to  try and drive down our in bodies and  operational carbon  from our developments and one triton  square was a really good candidate for  refurbishment  so it is a really big building it's  about 200 000 feet  on our campus near houston and we  committed to a major refurbishment and  aimed  for abraham outstanding target on this  building  and really we're just reincarnating that  building as a headquarters for a major  media company denso aegis  so that the building's um available for  for good use for the next 30 years  and what we found was in having a  demanding sustainable development brief  for the building  we saved um about 56  in embodied energy which is the  equivalent of 27 000 tons of carbon  we also saved about 43 of operating  energies the energy it takes to run the  building and that's the equivalent of  running  say 1400 homes in london for a year so  it was really good for the planet and  really good for us to have some very  stringent targets  and then um i think you mentioned cost  and um as commercial developers we keep  an eye on that too and  really by refurbishing we saved nearly  60 on  on cost versus what would have happened  if we'd knocked it down and  started again um and some of the really  amazing things about this building were  commitments to innovation so  during the process we saved 35 000 tons  of concrete  and 2 000 tons of steel which was the  equivalent of taking  6 000 lorries off london's roads versus  a new build juliet morgan really  interesting to hear thanks very much  indeed  now wimbledon begins today at the all  england tennis club and it's part of a  packed  summer of sport that will see hundreds  of thousands of fans  traveling to events around the world but  at what cost to the planet  building venues powering events  accommodating and feeding both players  and  fans all these things consume energy and  generate waste  which impacts the environment but now  some of the biggest competitions in the  world  are making changes in a bid to become  sustainable  sporting events well joining me now is  matthew campelli who's founder of the  sustainability report  welcome to you tell us what are the  biggest challenges  for sport when it comes to  sustainability is it the transport of  players and fans  what makes the biggest impact  so a lot of sports are now doing a lot  of things operationally around  sustainability which  which are really good and i think you'll  find that the wimbledon tennis  tournament will have a  net zero commitment and they'll be doing  lots of stuff around renewable energy  and around  biodiversity but the biggest challenges  sport tend to find  is around travel spectator travel uh  even travel of players and  and officials as well and also around  other scope 3  emissions around the value chain and  their suppliers  so you know that kind of the scope three  indirect emissions is what sport is  having a real challenge with addressing  at the moment  and do you feel that sports are becoming  increasingly aware of the impact that  they have on the planet  are things changing so i think there are  two things at play  sport is really feeling that um that is  being impacted by climate change  we see more extreme heat we're seeing  air pollution are affecting athletes  whether they're recreational or whether  they're professional  major storms and major major weather  events and at the same time sport knows  that it has to do stuff around  sustainability not just from a risk  management point of view  but also from a branding point of view  as well sport needs to be seen to be  doing  the right thing environmentally as well  as around social issues as well  so there's really two things driving it  currently and are there some sports  and actually some teams within those  sports that are  doing far better than others  yeah i mean absolutely we've got uh  teams like forest green rovers which i'm  sure many of your viewers would have  heard of the  the the fourth tier english football  league team which is  run by dale vince and doing an awful lot  around you know putting sustainability  at the heart of sport  but we're seeing more kind of mainstream  sports organizations like wimbledon for  example  like the international olympic committee  who are really putting climate targets  at the heart of their operations now  and i think while sport is gradually  moving in this direction  uh it'll be a little while before it's  mainstream across all sports but we're  going in the right  direction matthew campelli thanks very  much indeed  thank you well that's everything from us  for today coming up on the daily climate  show  tomorrow scientists are launching a  fleet of multi-million pound boys  off the coast of plymouth which aim to  collect data on our oceans  that's at the same time tomorrow here on  sky news see you then

